ustralia; daily versus discretionary use, with photoaging as the endpoint. The retrieved excerpt establishes design and population but not a facial-only regimen, exact application quantity, or Romanian UV context.\",\"comparability\":\"The trial's broad-spectrum SPF 15+ exposure is not a direct comparison of current facial formulas, cosmetic feel, or SPF tiers.\",\"confidence\":\"Moderate for daily broad-spectrum photoprotection as the routine anchor because of the RCT design; lower for a precise SPF/application rule because the directly extracted AAD passage was unavailable.\",\"disposition\":\"retain\"},{\"id\":\"a2\",\"slot\":\"actives\",\"finding\":\"For acne prevention/treatment, AAD guideline evidence strongly supports topical retinoids; salicylic acid is conditional rather than an equally supported core active.\",\"status\":\"sourced_claim\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(23)03389-3/fulltext\",\"title\":\"Guidelines of care for the management of acne vulgaris\",\"passage\":\"\\\"Strong recommendations are made for benzoyl peroxide, topical retinoids, topical antibiotics, and oral doxycycline.\\\" \\\"Conditional recommendations are made for topical clascoterone, salicylic acid, and azelaic acid.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Dermatology clinical guideline\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The guideline applies to acne vulgaris, not an asymptomatic normal-to-combination adult; it does not establish that an additional salicylic-acid product improves a routine already using a retinoid.\",\"method\":\"Evidence-based acne guideline using systematic review and GRADE, for adults, adolescents, and preadolescents with acne.\",\"comparability\":\"Acne-treatment recommendation strength does not prove photoaging benefit or superiority of a cosmetic retail formula.\",\"confidence\":\"High for relative guideline recommendation strength; moderate for transfer to prevention in a person without diagnosed acne.\",\"disposition\":\"qualify\"},{\"id\":\"a3\",\"slot\":\"actives\",\"finding\":\"Evidence for salicylic acid and topical niacinamide as acne treatments is limited/low certainty, so neither should be presented as a required substitute for a retinoid or as proven finished-product superiority.\",\"status\":\"sourced_claim\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7193765/\",\"title\":\"Topical azelaic acid, salicylic acid, nicotinamide, sulphur, zinc and fruit acid (alpha-hydroxy acid) for acne\",\"passage\":\"\\\"For PGA, there may be little or no difference between salicylic acid and tretinoin (RR 1.00, 95% CI 0.92 to 1.09; 1 study, 46 participants; low-quality evidence).\\\" \\\"Four studies evaluated nicotinamide against clindamycin or erythromycin, but none measured PGA.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Cochrane systematic review\",\"date\":\"Evidence current to May 2019\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"For salicylic acid versus benzoyl peroxide, the review reports no PGA measurement and very-low-certainty adverse-event evidence; for niacinamide, no included comparison measured PGA.\",\"method\":\"49 trials, 3880 reported participants across topical acne agents; pertinent comparisons were small and short, generally 5-8 weeks.\",\"comparability\":\"Neither evidence base establishes efficacy of a particular concentration, vehicle, or a combined retail formula. The general routine target is prevention, not necessarily active clinical acne.\",\"confidence\":\"High for the limitation, because it is explicit Cochrane grading; low for superiority or for choosing one specific concentration.\",\"disposition\":\"retain\"},{\"id\":\"a4\",\"slot\":\"actives\",\"finding\":\"Topical vitamin C can be considered optional rather than required: a systematic review found efficacy signals for uneven/wrinkled skin and depigmenting effects, but retrieved evidence did not establish a durable concentration-specific routine rule.\",\"status\":\"sourced_claim\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jocd.15748\",\"title\":\"Efficacy of topical vitamin C in melasma and photoaging: A systematic review\",\"passage\":\"\\\"Prospective, randomized controlled trials assessing protocols with topically applied vitamin C in patients with melasma or photodamage were searched in Medline, CENTRAL, and Scopus databases until the 12th of May 2022.\\\" \\\"This study revealed that vitamin C is effective in treating uneven, wrinkled skin and has depigmenting properties.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Systematic review\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The same retrieved search result states there had been a lack of studies evaluating topical vitamin C in photoaging and melasma; no direct concentration, vehicle, duration, or long-term passage was retrieved.\",\"method\":\"Systematic review of RCT protocols in melasma or photodamage, not an all-adult prevention trial.\",\"comparability\":\"Melasma/photodamage treatment populations and vitamin-C formulations are not directly comparable to a general preventive routine or a named retailer product.\",\"confidence\":\"Low-to-moderate: a systematic review supports optional use, but not a precise product-selection or sequencing claim.\",\"disposition\":\"qualify\"},{\"id\":\"a5\",\"slot\":\"actives\",\"finding\":\"Basic moisturization is reasonable for hydration/barrier support and retinoid tolerability, but retrieved evidence does not show that ceramides, glycerin, or a higher-priced formula is clinically superior for normal-to-combination adult facial skin.\",\"status\":\"inference\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://www.ijmpsjournal.com/article/a-systematic-review-of-facial-moisturizer\",\"title\":\"A Systematic Review of Facial Moisturizer\",\"passage\":\"\\\"Facial moisturizers are a crucial component of daily skincare and dermatological care because they improve skin hydration, prevent transepidermal water loss (TEWL), and maintain and restore the stratum corneum's (SC) barrier function.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Systematic review, search-result excerpt\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The stronger ceramide-specific search results concerned atopic dermatitis or senile xerosis, not the assigned normal-to-combination, no-dermatosis population.\",\"method\":\"General moisturizer mechanism; no direct retrieved normal-to-combination facial RCT comparing retailer formulations.\",\"comparability\":\"Disease-associated barrier impairment is not equivalent to ordinary adult skin or retinoid-induced irritation.\",\"confidence\":\"Moderate for selecting a simple moisturizer when dryness occurs; low for any ingredient or price-tier superiority.\",\"disposition\":\"qualify\"},{\"id\":\"s1\",\"slot\":\"safety\",\"finding\":\"Topical retinoids should be avoided in pregnancy/planning pregnancy; irritation, erythema, peeling, and dryness are material expected adverse effects. Breastfeeding safety remains uncertain in the retrieved review.\",\"status\":\"sourced_claim\",\"support\":[{\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8750127/\",\"title\":\"A Clinician's Guide to Topical Retinoids\",\"passage\":\"\\\"Topical retinoids are recommended to be avoided during pregnancy.\\\" \\\"Although there is evidence demonstrating that the risk may be minimal when using agents such as tretinoin, the risk still outweighs the benefits, and all retinoids should be avoided during pregnancy.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Clinical review\"},{\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8750127/\",\"title\":\"A Clinician's Guide to Topical Retinoids\",\"passage\":\"\\\"Tretinoin ... Side effects: Irritation, local dryness\\\"; \\\"Adapalene ... Irritation, Erythema, Peeling of the skin, Local dryness.\\\"\",\"sourceType\":\"Clinical review\"}],\"counterevidence\":\"The review reports minimal absorption and no demonstrated second/third-trimester risk in some studies, but still recommends avoidance because benefit does not justify uncertainty; breastfeeding excretion/use is explicitly unknown.\",\"method\":\"Clinical review of topical retinoi